 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Time Complexity:** $O(k)$ where $k$ is the number of occupied bricks intersected by the ray, plus the cost of traversing empty top-level cells. In sparse scenes, $k \ll N^3$ (where $N$ is the global resolution) [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Memory Complexity:** $O(B \cdot BRICK^3)$ where $B$ is the number of occupied bricks. For a heightmap stand-in with $N=128$, the brick count is roughly $top^2$ (~256 bricks) instead of the worst-case $top^3$ (4096) [S1, unofficial]. Memory savings grow with sparsity [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Time Complexity:** $O(k)$ where $k$ is the number of occupied bricks intersected by the ray, plus the cost of traversing empty top-level cells. In sparse scenes, $k \ll N^3$ (where $N$ is the global resolution).
+*   **Memory Complexity:** $O(B \cdot BRICK^3)$ where $B$ is the number of occupied bricks. For a heightmap stand-in with $N=128$, the brick count is roughly $top^2$ (~256 bricks) instead of the worst-case $top^3$ (4096) [S1, unofficial]. Memory savings grow with sparsity.
 *   **Measured Performance:** For drone airspace (>99% empty), the method significantly reduces traversal time compared to dense voxel buffers [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Strengths:** Significant memory reduction in sparse volumes; efficient skipping of empty space; compatible with GPU compute shaders for build phase [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Limitations:** Requires a build phase to construct the sparse structure; potential for visual artifacts at brick boundaries if not implemented carefully; increased $MAX\_STEPS$ may be required due to hierarchical overhead [S1, unofficial].
-*   **When to Use:** Sparse volumetric data (e.g., drone airspace, large outdoor scenes with sparse objects) [S1, unofficial].
-*   **When Not to Use:** Dense volumetric data (e.g., medical CT scans with high fill rate) where the overhead of hierarchical traversal may not justify the memory savings, or when dynamic updates are frequent and costly [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Strengths:** Significant memory reduction in sparse volumes; efficient skipping of empty space; compatible with GPU compute shaders for build phase.
+*   **Limitations:** Requires a build phase to construct the sparse structure; potential for visual artifacts at brick boundaries if not implemented carefully; increased $MAX\_STEPS$ may be required due to hierarchical overhead.
+*   **When to Use:** Sparse volumetric data (e.g., drone airspace, large outdoor scenes with sparse objects).
+*   **When Not to Use:** Dense volumetric data (e.g., medical CT scans with high fill rate) where the overhead of hierarchical traversal may not justify the memory savings, or when dynamic updates are frequent and costly.
 
 ## Background
-Hierarchical Brick-Map DDA was introduced as part of the ResQ Viz project, specifically in PR #67, to optimize ray marching for sparse volumes like drone airspace [S1, unofficial]. It builds upon the concept of sparse voxel traversal and brick-based storage, adapting it for GPU-based ray marching with WebGPU [S1, unofficial]. The method addresses the inefficiency of dense voxel buffers in highly sparse environments by introducing a two-level hierarchy: a coarse top-level grid and fine-grained bricks [S1, unofficial].
